              "text": "{\n  \"query_text\": \"According to WHO guidelines, what are the specific daily intake recommendations for fruits and vegetables for adults and children of different age groups, and how does consuming these fresh, unrefined foods help prevent cardiovascular conditions and stroke based on their potassium content?\",\n  \"target_population\": [\n    \"adults\",\n    \"children (2-9 years old)\",\n    \"adolescents (10 years and older)\"\n  ],\n  \"conditions\": [\n    \"hypertension\",\n    \"cardiovascular diseases\",\n    \"stroke\"\n  ],\n  \"draft_rationale\": \"To answer the query, the model must synthesize information from both the current 2023 carbohydrate guideline and the 2012 potassium guideline. From the 2023 guideline, it must extract the specific daily fruit and vegetable intake recommendations: at least 400 g for adults and children/adolescents aged 10 or older, at least 350 g for children aged 6-9, and at least 250 g for children aged 2-5. From the 2012 guideline, it must explain that fruits and vegetables are key unrefined sources of potassium (which is reduced by food processing), and that adequate potassium intake is protective against hypertension and cardiovascular diseases, and is inversely associated with the risk of stroke.\"\n}",
